      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z.$",
      "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {z}^{0}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{z}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot z+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$",
